<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Holkars</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Holkars were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> ruling family who governed Indore in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. They were one of the major chiefly houses, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scindias">Scindias</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaekwads">Gaekwads</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhonsles">Bhonsles</a>, that held power in the Maratha confederacy. Factionalism among these chiefs weakened central authority and contributed to the decline of the Maratha Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holkars.jpg" alt="Holkars" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_Indore_State,_1908.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Chhatrapati at Satara to the Peshwa's administration at Pune, which supervised a confederacy of semi-autonomous chiefs, among them the Gaekwads of Baroda, <b>Holkars</b> of Indore, Scindias of Gwalior and Bhonsles of Nagpur, who collected revenue and maintained armies while acknowledging the Peshwa's leadership and the Chhatrapati's formal sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1730s the Marathas under the Peshwa Baji Rao I and later the Scindia and <b>Holkar</b> houses exacted heavy tribute, nobles feuded among themselves and the treasury was exhausted; in 1810 the young princess Krishna Kumari was poisoned to avert a war between rival suitors from Jaipur…</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Damaji Rao II took Baroda (Vadodara) and much of the province, and the Gaekwads became one of the great Maratha houses alongside the Peshwas, <b>Holkars</b> and Scindias.</blockquote><a href="/state/baroda-state">Baroda State</a></li><li><blockquote>Dhar was badly weakened by the wars of Scindia and <b>Holkar</b> around 1800 and recovered under British protection after the treaty of 1819.</blockquote><a href="/state/dhar-state">Dhar State</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Holkars</b> became one of the main Maratha houses; Ahilyabai later made Maheshwar on the Narmada their seat of government.</blockquote><a href="/state/indore-state">Indore State</a></li><li><blockquote>Abdul Ghafur Khan, a Pathan cavalry commander and brother-in-law of Amir Khan, the founder of Tonk State, received Jaora and nearby lands from the <b>Holkars</b> of Indore State around 1808.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaora-state">Jaora State</a></li></ul></section>
